 "standard_text": "Estimate, measure, record, compare and order capacity and volume using appropriate measuring instruments; use millilitres (mℓ) and litres (ℓ); calculate and solve problems involving capacity and volume, including problems with decimal fractions to 2 decimal places; convert between litres and millilitres, including conversions with decimal fractions.",
 "meta_desc": "Free CAPS Grade 6 capacity and volume worksheets: millilitres and litres, converting with decimals, cm³ and problems. 100 questions with memos.",
 "key_big": "1 ℓ = 1 000 mℓ.",
 "key_sub": "1 cm³ holds 1 mℓ.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How to think about it</h2><ul><li>1 ℓ = 1 000 mℓ.</li><li>To change ℓ to mℓ, multiply by 1 000: 1,5 ℓ = 1 500 mℓ. To change mℓ to ℓ, divide by 1 000.</li><li>Capacity is how much a container can hold. Volume is the space an object takes up.</li><li>1 cm³ holds 1 mℓ, so a 1 000 cm³ box holds 1 ℓ.</li></ul><h2>Examples</h2><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>A 2 ℓ jug fills cups of 250 mℓ</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>2 ℓ = 2 000 mℓ</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>2 000 ÷ 250</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 8 cups</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><div class='sn-tw'><table class='sn-table'><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>What to do instead</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>100 mℓ in a litre</td><td>1 ℓ = 1 000 mℓ.</td></tr><tr><td>The comma the wrong way</td><td>ℓ is bigger, so the number is smaller.</td></tr><tr><td>Capacity or volume?</td><td>Capacity: what it holds. Volume: space it takes up.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
